WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.857
When you eat chicken, eggs, beans, or another protein food, you're eating proteins made by another living organism.

2
00:00:12.857 --> 00:00:21.429
Each is a large molecule built from smaller molecules called amino acids.

3
00:00:21.429 --> 00:00:28.571
There are 20 common amino acids used to build proteins.

4
00:00:28.571 --> 00:00:35.714
They are linked in a particular order by peptide bonds.

5
00:00:35.714 --> 00:00:46.429
Two linked amino acids make a dipeptide, three a tripeptide, and longer chains are polypeptides.

6
00:00:46.429 --> 00:00:57.857
A protein is not just a mixture of these amino acids; it has a specific sequence.

7
00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:00.000
That sequence matters.

8
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:30.797
Every amino acid has a similar core structure, including a nitrogen-containing amino group, but one part varies: its side chain, also called its R group.

9
00:01:30.797 --> 00:01:40.652
Some side chains attract water; others avoid water.

10
00:01:40.652 --> 00:01:48.043
Some carry positive or negative charges.

11
00:01:48.043 --> 00:02:07.754
As a protein chain forms, these properties influence how parts of it interact, bend, and fold.

12
00:02:07.754 --> 00:02:17.609
The order determines which side chains can interact.

13
00:02:17.609 --> 00:02:25.000
Folding gives proteins their three-dimensional structures.

14
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.378
Why does the shape matter?

15
00:02:28.378 --> 00:02:33.784
Imagine an enzyme that binds a particular molecule.

16
00:02:33.784 --> 00:02:41.216
Its folded shape creates a binding site where that molecule fits.

17
00:02:41.216 --> 00:02:50.676
If the amino-acid sequence changes, the shape or binding properties can sometimes change too.

18
00:02:50.676 --> 00:03:09.595
In sickle cell disease, one amino-acid substitution in hemoglobin creates a different surface property that promotes hemoglobin fibers under low oxygen, making red blood cells stiff and sickle-shaped.

19
00:03:09.595 --> 00:03:15.000
The big connection is sequence, shape, and function.

20
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.816
Now follow that protein after you eat it.

21
00:03:22.816 --> 00:03:30.632
In your stomach, hydrochloric acid can denature it.

22
00:03:30.632 --> 00:03:42.356
That means the protein unfolds or loses much of its working shape.

23
00:03:42.356 --> 00:03:52.126
But the amino acids are still linked along the chain.

24
00:03:52.126 --> 00:04:05.805
Pepsin, active in the acidic stomach, begins cutting peptide bonds to form shorter chains.

25
00:04:05.805 --> 00:04:23.391
In the small intestine, pancreatic and intestinal proteases break those chains into smaller peptides and free amino acids.

26
00:04:23.391 --> 00:04:33.161
Unfolding is not the same thing as cutting peptide bonds.

27
00:04:33.161 --> 00:04:40.000
The intact food protein is being dismantled.

28
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.971
The small intestine absorbs free amino acids using transport proteins.

29
00:04:47.971 --> 00:05:03.116
Some dipeptides and tripeptides use a transporter called PEPT1 and are generally broken into amino acids inside intestinal cells.

30
00:05:03.116 --> 00:05:21.449
Amino acids then move into the blood, travel first through the hepatic portal circulation to the liver, and become available to cells elsewhere.

31
00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:35.000
The body is receiving useful raw materials, not a ready-made copy of the protein from the meal.

32
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:47.945
What determines the new protein a cell will make?

33
00:05:47.945 --> 00:06:00.890
DNA inside the nucleus contains genes: instructions for proteins.

34
00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:13.836
Cells do not read every gene all the time.

35
00:06:13.836 --> 00:06:36.849
Internal and external signals help control which genes are active and how much product is needed.

36
00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:56.986
When a gene is active, the cell transcribes an mRNA copy of its instructions.

37
00:06:56.986 --> 00:07:20.000
The DNA stays in the nucleus; the mRNA exits and carries the instructions into the cytoplasm.

38
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:25.519
The mRNA reaches a ribosome.

39
00:07:25.519 --> 00:07:34.351
The ribosome reads its code in three-base codons.

40
00:07:34.351 --> 00:07:46.494
Transfer RNA, or tRNA, brings matching amino acids to the ribosome.

41
00:07:46.494 --> 00:08:00.844
The ribosome connects those amino acids in the order specified by the mRNA.

42
00:08:00.844 --> 00:08:10.779
The chain emerges and folds into a new protein.

43
00:08:10.779 --> 00:08:45.000
Notice: even if some of these amino acids originally came from chicken or beans, the newly made protein's sequence comes from the body's DNA—not from the order in the food protein.

44
00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.845
Consider insulin.

45
00:08:46.845 --> 00:09:14.515
If you swallow ordinary insulin, the digestive tract treats it like a protein: it is unfolded and broken down, so it does not simply reach the bloodstream as working insulin.

46
00:09:14.515 --> 00:09:28.350
Absorbing its amino acids does not tell your body to assemble them back into insulin.

47
00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:49.563
Instead, specialized pancreatic beta cells make insulin using the instructions in their own insulin gene, in response to signals such as blood glucose.

48
00:09:49.563 --> 00:10:01.553
The same broad digestion principle applies to bovine growth hormone, also a protein.

49
00:10:01.553 --> 00:10:11.699
Eating the hormone does not instruct your cells to recreate it.

50
00:10:11.699 --> 00:10:20.000
Food supplies the amino acids; DNA supplies the instructions.

51
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:26.250
Amino acids also have other possible fates.

52
00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:44.107
They help make other needed compounds; and when used as fuel, they generally undergo deamination, removing the nitrogen-containing amino group.

53
00:10:44.107 --> 00:10:53.036
The liver converts nitrogen waste to urea, which kidneys excrete.

54
00:10:53.036 --> 00:11:07.321
Remaining carbon skeletons can provide energy or make other fuels, including glucose from certain amino acids.

55
00:11:07.321 --> 00:11:20.714
Energy from protein can contribute to body-fat storage if overall energy intake consistently exceeds needs.

56
00:11:20.714 --> 00:11:29.643
There is no special storage depot for excess amino acids.

57
00:11:29.643 --> 00:11:50.179
To recap: the food protein gets broken down, its amino acids are absorbed, and body cells make new proteins according to genetic instructions.

58
00:11:50.179 --> 00:12:00.000
The sequence shapes the protein; its shape helps determine its function.
